    "blurb": "Put a policy (a rule made by leaders) on trial. The people of 2050 argue against it.",
    "from": "“The Future Generations Trial: What Future Did You Promise Us?” at Dubai Future Week on 20 November. It is a pretend courtroom. Young people speak for future generations (people who will live later). Leaders explain why today’s decisions are right. A jury of different ages decides.",
    "drop": "The decisions we make today will still affect people in 2050. In this courtroom, the people of 2050 can reply.",
    "example": "Here is a different policy to show how each side argues. “Schools may replace PE lessons with extra computer lessons.” <b>Prosecution (the side against the policy), speaking from 2050:</b> “In 2050 this means adults who sat still through eleven years of school. You gave us more screen time. You took away the habit of moving.” <b>Defence (the side for it), speaking today:</b> “By 2050 most jobs need digital (computer) skills. Pupils who had more time to learn them are better prepared. They can still play sport after school.”",
    "make": "A verdict sheet. A verdict is the jury’s decision. The sheet shows the decision and the strongest argument heard. It also shows the amendment (a change to the policy). Each person can enter their own sheet.",
